# Gate 2's ratchet floor. See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-phase2-go-relay-design.md
# (Stage 2a > Gate 2) and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-10-phase2-2a7-coverage-gate.md.
#
# `missing` is the ratchet -- a MAXIMUM, not a target, and lower is better. It is set
# from the WORST run of at least twelve on the tree that earned it, which is why this
# gate carries no separate tolerance parameter: the measured spread is already inside
# the number. **104 statements flap across this campaign's own 12 CI rounds** (below),
# measured FIRST-HAND from the retained per-label `gate-data-*` artifacts of the same
# 12 runs `missing` was set from -- not from a smaller sample, not from an earlier tree,
# and not inherited from a citation. The dominant source is `cleanup_task`
# (`server.py`, ~53 of the 104, in four clusters: 1961-1989, 2014-2046, 2063-2068,
# 2166-2192) -- unsurprising, since the spec's own § Reachability already names it as
# "the source of the coverage gate's own run-to-run variance". The rest: `server.py`'s
# `extend_ownership` (581-587) and `_recover_stuck_channel_stops` (1782-1788), plus
# assorted singles/pairs at 1263-1264, 1615, 1806-1807, 1940, 2274-2275, 2288, 2440;
# `input/manager.py` at 363-365, 640-641, 1009-1011, 1383-1384, 1785, 1856-1863 (17
# total, NOT including the 1739-1767 stderr-reader join, which does not flap on this
# tree); `input/http_streamer.py` 156-157; `live_proxy/utils.py` 221-222; one line
# each in `input/buffer.py` (3 total: 282, 355-356), `output/fmp4/manager.py` (175-176,
# 334), `output/fmp4/buffer.py` (92), `output/fmp4/generator.py` (142),
# `output/profile/manager.py` (382), `output/ts/generator.py` (635).
#
# THIS SUPERSEDES BOTH the four-region list this file previously carried (owner-side
# coordinated stop, non-owner local cleanup, the stderr-reader join,
# `_wait_for_channel_ready`'s error branch) AND a plan-review correction to it measured
# at `04841a47`, before this PR's ~200 tests existed -- two of them (Task 9b, Task 10)
# land squarely on the modules in question, so a citation measured before they landed
# could have been invalidated by them. It was not: this measurement is taken from the
# SAME 12 runs `missing` was set from, i.e. AFTER Tasks 1-11 (Task 9b and Task 10
# included) were already committed, and it independently confirms the citation's
# claims -- the owner-side coordinated stop (`channel_service.py`, zero flap observed)
# and the stderr-reader join (`input/manager.py:1739-1767`, zero flap observed) do not
# flap on this tree, `_wait_for_channel_ready`'s error branch does not appear at all,
# and `non-owner local cleanup` (`server.py:2099-2112`, `2489-2566`) is exactly constant
# across all 12 rounds -- zero flap observed there either. So this is a first-hand
# CONFIRMATION of the correction, not merely an inherited claim, and it is stronger
# evidence than the citation because it was measured on the post-PR tree, in CI, over
# more rounds (12 vs whatever `04841a47` used).
#
# CAVEAT, stated because the shape of this note is what let the OLD list go stale
# unnoticed: unobserved-to-vary across 12 rounds is not proof of stability, only
# absence of observed variance in that sample. This list has now changed three times
# (the original set, the 2a-7 four-region list, this one) -- exactly why the gate
# carries no tolerance parameter keyed to a fixed region list, and why a future
# correction to THIS list, when it comes, should re-derive from CI artifacts again
# rather than assume today's set is permanent. An independent local 6-round check
# (per-container isolation, same tree, kept in this PR's own record) broadly
# corroborates the shape of this list -- non-owner local cleanup and the owner-side
# coordinated stop stayed constant there too -- but is not the source of the numbers
# above; the source is the 12 CI rounds.
#
# `shape`, `modules` and `rcfile` are EQUALITY checks, not comparisons. A different
# measurement shape, module list or measurement DEFINITION makes two numbers
# incomparable rather than better or worse, and the gate says so instead of ratcheting.
#
# `modules` is a sha256 (first 12 hex chars) of the sorted list of repo-relative paths
# in coverage's JSON `files` report -- i.e. of WHICH files are in scope, not how big
# they are -- with the full sorted list committed alongside this file in
# scripts/coverage_live_path.floor.modules so a --gate mismatch can name the files
# added and removed, not just show two unequal hashes. `module_count` is that list's
# length, printed for a human skimming the two numbers. gate() verifies the companion
# file against `modules` on EVERY run, independent of the current coverage data,
# because the two are written in separate steps by --write-floor and could otherwise
# drift silently -- see scripts/coverage_live_path.sh's hash_file_list().
#
# `rcfile` is a sha256 (first 12 hex chars) of scripts/coverage_live_path.coveragerc's
# raw BYTES, not its resolved file set -- it exists because `modules` alone is blind to
# an `exclude_lines` addition or an `[report] omit` narrowing, either of which can move
# `statements` (and correspondingly `missing`, since an excluded line counts as neither
# covered nor missed) without adding or removing a single file. That is the cheapest
# possible way to make the ratchet easier, and this is what catches it. Tripping on a
# comment-only edit to the rcfile is an accepted false positive: that file is edited
# rarely and deliberately, and a spurious re-baseline there costs one
# `--write-floor --shape-only` run, not a fresh CI census (see HOW TO MOVE THIS FLOOR
# below).
#
# `statements` is NOT compared -- it is recorded provenance only, printed alongside the
# ratchet for information. It moves whenever ANY statement is added to or removed from
# an ALREADY-INCLUDED module (measured, statically, via PythonParser: one three-line
# function added to apps/proxy/control_plane.py took it 124 -> 127 statements) -- not
# only when the rcfile's module list changes -- so comparing it for equality would have
# fired on ordinary code changes to files already in scope, not just on scope changes.
# It never actually did: every 2a PR added only test files, which the rcfile omits, so
# the denominator never moved in practice -- the bug was latent, caught before a 2b PR
# that touches included production modules directly could trip it. `modules` and
# `rcfile` are what actually answer "did the denominator's composition or definition
# change", which is the question this guard exists to ask.
#
# Written by:  scripts/coverage_live_path.sh --write-floor
# Enforced by: scripts/coverage_live_path.sh --gate  (backend-tests.yml, job coverage-gate)
#
# PROVENANCE (Phase 2 PR 2b-4, Task 13 -- measured in CI, where the gate is
# enforced). `missing` below is the WORST of 12 rounds of backend-tests.yml's
# own coverage-label + coverage-gate jobs, on migration/phase2b-coverage-floor
# at 22d684974f08c4cc8f183e5af2d06a95e895964a, 2026-09-13. Full sequence, in
# order:
#   1513 1525 1501 1510 1510 1510 1496 1519 1514 1509 1504 1512
# max=1525 (round 2)  min=1496 (round 7)  spread=29  n=12
# The stopping rule (maximum unchanged for >=6 consecutive rounds, minimum 12)
# was met at round 12: the max was set at round 2 and held for the remaining
# ten rounds. `statements` (8073) is unchanged from the previous floor's
# denominator -- this PR's tests only, no production module gained or lost a
# line in a way that moved the file set or the rcfile.
#
# THIS IS A DELIBERATE, LARGE LOWERING (2050 -> 1525), not a re-baseline: 2b-4
# is the coverage-floor PR the 2a-7 floor's own header named as the shortfall's
# owner (Stage 2a ended at 74.30%, not the spec's >=80%; D7 blocked every 2c
# PR until this closed). 481 new-test statements closed a measured 388-
# statement shortfall against a ceiling of 1614 (= 8073 - ceil(0.8 * 8073)),
# selected from the plan's 381-400 band (Tasks 3-11 plus 8b/9b in scope from
# the start) before any test was written, not sized to a total after the
# fact. Margin at M_final: 1614 - 1525 = 89.
#
# LOCAL VS CI, AND WHY THE HISTORICAL +35 DELTA IS NOT CARRIED FORWARD. The
# 2a-7 floor above was set in CI after its LOCAL campaign's maximum (2044) was
# exceeded by the very first CI run (2045), and the 2b-4 plan sized its test
# scope's cushion assuming that same failure mode -- roughly local+35 in CI --
# would recur. It did not: this campaign's own local isolated-container
# reconciliation ran 1484-1512, and the CI census above ran 1496-1525; at the
# maximum the delta was +13, not +35. Recorded here, not just in the PR, so
# the next stage does not inherit a delta that has now failed to reproduce
# once. The mechanism the 2a-7 note names (per-container isolation makes the
# owner-side block deterministic but not the total) is unchanged in KIND --
# some regions still flap -- but not in the SPECIFIC regions: see the
# 104-statement flappy set measured directly from this campaign's own 12
# retained CI artifacts, at the top of this file. Only the SIZE of the
# local/CI gap varied between campaigns, which is itself evidence the gap is
# not a fixed constant to budget against, and the correct response stays
# "measure in CI", never "assume a fixed offset".
#
# TWO ROUNDS IN THIS CENSUS CARRY COSMETIC DAMAGE UNRELATED TO THE COVERAGE
# FIGURE. Round 5's overall workflow-run conclusion reads `cancelled` even
# though all 22 of its jobs -- every `Coverage *` label, `Coverage gate` (the
# source of its 1510), and `Backend result`, the aggregate job that runs
# AFTER the gate -- completed with `success`; the run's own top-level
# conclusion is not a validity signal for this measurement, only the job set
# is (see scripts/coverage_live_path_isolated.sh's own #266-descended
# reasoning, applied one level up). Round 9 carries one cancelled job
# (`core.tests`, a plain backend-matrix label unrelated to coverage) and one
# downstream `Backend result` failure, both caused by this campaign's own
# serial dispatch loop: it advances to round N+1 as soon as round N's
# `Coverage gate` job concludes, which is before every job in round N's run
# finishes, so the ref's cancel-in-progress concurrency group clips whatever
# is still running in round N once round N+1 is dispatched. This can only
# ever clip a job downstream of the gate, never a coverage job itself
# (coverage-gate needs [coverage-label], so every coverage job is already
# complete by the time it runs) -- harmless for this census, but it is why
# most of these 12 runs are not clean runs for any other purpose, and it is
# recorded here so a reader of the run history is not left wondering why a
# `missing=` figure came from a run GitHub calls cancelled or failed.
#
# PRIOR PROVENANCE (Phase 2 PR 2a-7, amended before merge -- measured in CI, where the
# gate is enforced). `missing` below is the WORST of 15 rounds of
# backend-tests.yml's own coverage-label + coverage-gate jobs, on this branch,
# 2026-09-11. Full sequence, in order:
#   2045 2035 2050 2048 2031 2024 2028 2033 2017 2019 2016 2045 2017 2035 2037
# max=2050 (round 3)  min=2016 (round 11)  spread=34  n=15
# The stopping rule (maximum unchanged for >=6 consecutive rounds, minimum 12) was
# met at round 12 and held to 15.
#
# WHY THIS REPLACES THE 2044 BELOW, AND WHY 2044 WAS NOT WRONG. The earlier campaign
# was a correct 21-round census that met the same stopping rule -- in LOCAL
# containers. This gate runs in CI, and the first CI run on the merge-ready tree
# measured 2045: one statement over a floor whose whole claim is that the spread is
# already inside it. The floor was measured in one environment and enforced in
# another, and the local maximum understated the CI distribution by 6. Re-measuring
# HERE is the fix; widening 2044 by a tolerance would have been the tempting one,
# and is exactly the parameter this file exists to avoid.
#
# It is variance, not a regression: the very next CI run measured 2035, comfortably
# under 2044, and the census min is 2016 -- better than anything the local campaign
# saw. Attributed per LINE across six of these runs (the method this file's own gate
# message prescribes -- diff the missing-line SETS, never difference totals): 92
# lines flap, 64 of them in server.py alone, with input/manager.py contributing 13
# (including 1339-1340, the #231 pair) and fmp4/generator.py 4. That is the same
# bimodal block-flipping the local campaign found, sampled in a noisier scheduler.
#
# ORIGINAL LOCAL CAMPAIGN, kept because it is the tree-to-tree comparison and the
# denominator evidence, and because a floor that quietly forgets its earlier number
# cannot be audited (Phase 2 PR 2a-7, Task 7).
#
# (Task 7, local). `missing` below is the WORST of 21 rounds
# under the per-container/v1-sysmon shape, NOT the figure --write-floor's own run
# happened to produce (2009) and NOT a median -- hand-set after the write, per the
# plan's Task 7 Step 4. Tree: 41f8021a12e3210d50ee4871cbdfecc1647aa89c (2a-7's branch,
# all four coverage PRs plus Tasks 1-7 committed). Measured 2026-09-10 against
# dispatcharr-testrunner-2a7-{proxy,liveproxy,channels}, three containers, one label
# each. Stopping rule (maximum unchanged for >=6 consecutive rounds, minimum 12) was
# met at round 12 and held through round 20; rounds continued to 20 for margin, per
# the same over-run judgement Task 1 used. Full sequence, in order:
#   2041 2005 2026 2039 2024 2035 2008 2044 2013 2032 2028
#   2026 2003 2026 2033 2025 2028 2028 2029 2023 2023
# max=2044 (round 7)  min=2003 (round 12)  spread=41  n=21
# The tree's denominator (7978) is unchanged from Task 1's merged-tree measurement,
# but this is a DIFFERENT measurement, not a re-run of it: Task 1's floor candidate
# was 2048 on the tree before Tasks 3/4 added two tests (row 29's and row 30's), and
# this one is 2044 -- 4 statements lower, on a tree with slightly more covered, not a
# discrepancy between two measurements of the same thing.
#
# HOW TO MOVE THIS FLOOR (read before running --write-floor).
#
# THESE ARE TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF MOVE, and only one of them needs the campaign
# below. A `missing` move changes what the ratchet PERMITS and can only be justified
# by a real measurement of the spread -- that is steps 1-5. A `shape`/`modules`/
# `rcfile`-only re-baseline (the module list or the rcfile changed, deliberately, and
# `missing` is UNCHANGED from what the previous floor already recorded) is not a claim
# about the spread at all -- it is telling the gate "the thing being measured is
# different now, here is its new identity." That needs exactly ONE clean local or CI
# run and a single `--write-floor --shape-only`, WITHOUT steps 1, 2, 4 or 5's
# multi-round census: there is no new `missing` value to earn through repeated
# measurement, because none is being claimed, and `--shape-only` cannot write one --
# it updates `shape`, `modules`, `module_count` and `rcfile` only, and leaves
# `statements`, `missing`, `percent`, `measured` and `runs` byte-identical to
# whatever they already were. Say which kind of move this is in the PR either way --
# "re-baselined with --shape-only, missing unchanged" is a one-line, one-run PR;
# "raised the floor to N, campaign attached" is not.
#
# `--shape-only` is the answer to a question this file's own plain `--write-floor`
# already declined once (see step 4 below): can the tool do this safely without a
# hand-edit? The two are not the same request. Plain `--write-floor` was asked to
# accept an explicit `missing` VALUE, which has to thread through the regression
# check (which run's figure counts as "worse than the floor"?) and the percent
# computation (recomputed against which value?) -- real machinery, declined on
# purpose. `--shape-only` writes FEWER fields instead: it never touches `missing` or
# `percent` at all, so neither question has anywhere to attach. A hand-edit procedure
# for this case was tried first and rejected: a maintainer following prose instead of
# running one command is the failure mode this guard exists to prevent one level
# further up, and the two ways to get it wrong are silent and both green -- run plain
# `--write-floor` for a shape-only move and the campaign's `missing`/`runs`
# provenance is silently overwritten by whatever one run just produced (measured:
# 2050->2006, runs 15->0, on a real floor, in the course of writing this fix); hand-
# restore those fields afterward and forget, and nothing checks that you did.
#
# What follows is the multi-round procedure for when `missing` itself is moving.
# `--write-floor` (without `--shape-only`) writes the figure from the run it JUST
# took -- one run, not the worst of N -- because the floor policy (worst of >=12
# rounds under a stopping rule; see the top of this file) lives in the stopping-rule
# PROCEDURE, not in the script. The two are not the same thing by design, and
# `--write-floor` cannot know it is being run for the Nth time in a campaign versus
# once, so it never guesses at "worst so far" on your behalf. The exact sequence,
# every time `missing` legitimately moves:
#   1. Measure IN CI, not only locally: dispatch backend-tests.yml on the branch
#      repeatedly and read `this run missing=` from each coverage-gate job, until the
#      MAXIMUM has been unchanged for >=6 consecutive rounds, >=12 total. Record every
#      round, in order -- a bare min/max cannot show the rule was met. Local rounds via
#      scripts/coverage_live_path_isolated.sh --report are still worth taking (they are
#      far faster to iterate on, and they attribute a change per file), but they do NOT
#      set the number: this floor was first set from a 21-round LOCAL census whose
#      maximum the very first CI run exceeded. The floor must be measured where it is
#      enforced. Note backend-tests.yml's concurrency group is per-ref with
#      cancel-in-progress, so CI rounds on one branch are sequential, not parallel.
#   2. Take max(runs). That number, not any single run's own figure, is what belongs
#      in `missing` below.
#   3. Run plain --write-floor once (writable checkout or a scratch rw container --
#      /repo is read-only in the standard test-hook container) to get the correct
#      FILE FORMAT, shape, statements, modules, module_count and rcfile fields
#      machine-produced -- and scripts/coverage_live_path.floor.modules rewritten to
#      match. Commit that file alongside this one; it is what lets a future --gate
#      mismatch name files, not just hashes.
#   4. Hand-edit `missing` to max(runs) from step 2 if it differs from what
#      --write-floor's own run produced (it usually will -- one run is rarely the
#      worst of N), and recompute `percent` = (1 - missing/statements) * 100 to match.
#      This hand-edit is the honest, documented step for THIS case, not a workaround:
#      teaching plain --write-floor to accept an explicit `missing` override was
#      considered for this PR and declined, for the threading reasons above -- a
#      maintainer action taken rarely and by hand, with no shape-only shortcut
#      available because a real `missing` move has no "fewer fields" version.
#   5. Re-run --gate from the ordinary READ-ONLY test-hook container -- the shape CI
#      actually runs it in -- and confirm it exits 0 against the new floor before
#      committing.
shape=per-container/v1-sysmon
statements=8073
missing=1525
percent=81.11
measured=2026-09-13
runs=12
modules=8cb5c65dac3e
module_count=38
rcfile=3af0a78b9b6f
